🚚 WGUPS Routing Program

This project is a routing optimization simulation for the Western Governors University Parcel Service (WGUPS). It models daily deliveries in Salt Lake City and generates efficient routes for three trucks and two drivers carrying 40 packages.

The goal is to deliver all packages on time while keeping the total distance traveled under 140 miles.

📦 Problem Scenario

Each truck can carry up to 16 packages.

Trucks travel at 18 mph (no stops for fuel).

Deliveries start no earlier than 8:00 AM.

Loading/unloading is assumed instantaneous.

Some packages include special notes (e.g., package #9 has an incorrect address until 10:20 AM).

Supervisors need to track package ID, status, delivery time, and truck location.

🧠 Approach

Used a Greedy Nearest-Neighbor algorithm to optimize routes.

Built a hash table for quick package lookups by ID.

Simulated time progression, updating truck positions and package statuses (At Hub → En Route → Delivered).

Added logic for constraints like truck capacity, delivery deadlines, and corrected addresses.

🛠️ Tech Stack

Language: Python

Data Structures: Hash Tables, Graphs, Queues

Algorithms: Greedy Nearest-Neighbor, Graph traversal

📊 Key Features

Optimized Routing: Combined mileage under 140 miles.

Deadline-Aware: Prioritizes packages with strict deadlines.

Supervisor Dashboard (CLI): Query package status, truck progress, and mileage.

Error Handling: Handles special delivery notes and address changes.

▶️ Usage

From the command line you can:

Track all packages at a given time.

Look up an individual package by ID.

See per-truck mileage and delivery progress.

📈 Results

All 40 packages delivered on time.

Total mileage ≈ 139 miles.

Design can scale to other cities with minor changes.
